  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 208 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warm front lifting north across New York this morning \nwill be the catalyst for showers and thunderstorm development as we \nhead into the mid afternoon hours and well into the evening. The \nlatest high-resolution guidance has slowed the onset of \nprecipitation into our forecast area until around 5 PM across far \nwestern portions of northern New York with rain and thunderstorms \nnot entering Vermont until after 8 PM. The best instability doesn't \ndevelop until after 00Z as mid-leve lapse rates begin to steepen. \nAnywhere from 1000 to 1500 J/kg of MLCAPE is forecasted to develop \nwhich will be coupled with 40 knots of 0-6km shear. This should help \nproduce some strong to localized severe thunderstorms and SPC has \nplaced a marginal risk for severe weather across northern New York \nand the northern half of Vermont due to the increasing instability. \nThe lack of surface convergence should keep the areal coverage low \nbut it wouldn't be surprising to see some stronger storms following \nsunset tonight. In addition, we could see some impressive \ninstantaneous rainfall rates with PWATS above the 80th percentile so \nwe can't rule out some ponding of water on roads to localized \nflooding. These storms will be moving quickly (40 knots of 0-6 \nshear) which will mitigate the flood threat but will also help to \nmove these storms east of the Connecticut river shortly after \nmidnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Outside of a start shower across the Adirondacks or \nGreen Mountains, quiet and dry weather is expected on both Tuesday \nand Wednesday. Weak surface high pressure will try to build north \nbut will generally be suppressed due to low pressure to our north and \neast. Nevertheless, we should see some clearing in the skies midweek \nwith Wednesday showing partly to mostly sunny skies for the partial \neclipse. Temperatures will be in the mid 70s to lower 80s with winds \ngenerally from the west/northwest at 10 mph or less. Patchy dense \nfog will be possible in the favorable river valleys Wednesday \nmorning given the upcoming rainfall this afternoon, light winds, and \nclearing skies.\n\nThe next system will move through the region on Thursday as a strong \ncold front approaches the region. The frontal timing has slowed \nslightly in the last 24 hours with the frontal passage now expected \nto be closer to peak heating. This has caused both the AI and \nmachine learning algorithms to slightly increase the severe \npotential for Thursday. We will once again be working with 1000 to \n1500 J/kg of ML CAPE and 40 knots of 0-6 km shear which is more than \nsufficient to see organized convection capable of producing strong \nto locally severe storms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: The upper level low will close off to our east Friday \nmorning which will help bring some recycled maritime into our region \nunder an easterly flow regime. This will likely spark additional \nrain showers across the northern and western slopes of the Green \nMountains with upslope conditions. However, it seems likely that \nmost valley locations will generally be on the drier side on Friday. \nDeep layer ridging will build across the region late Friday through \nthe weekend with dry, seasonably cool, and less humid air expected. \nHighs should top out in the mid to upper 70s with dewpoints dropping \ninto the 40s each afternoon and plentiful sunshine.",
